Nmap Scan Types: How They Work and What They Leave in Packets and Logs
1. Port states Nmap reports
State
Meaning
open
A service is listening and answered.
closed
Reachable, but no service is listening.
filtered
Nmap cannot tell, because a firewall is blocking or dropping the probe.
unfiltered
Reachable, but open or closed is unknown (seen with an ACK scan, -sA).
open|filtered
No response, so it could be open or filtered (common in UDP, NULL, FIN and Xmas scans).
closed|filtered
Nmap cannot decide between closed and filtered (rare).


2. The seven scan options
Option
Name
How it works
What it leaves in packets
What it leaves in logs / detection
-sn
Ping scan (host discovery, no port scan)
On the local network it sends ARP requests. Across routers it sends ICMP echo, a TCP SYN to port 443 and a TCP ACK (or SYN when not root) to port 80. A reply means the host is up.
A rapid series of ARP requests or ICMP echoes to consecutive addresses.
Rarely logged by hosts. Firewalls and IDS can alert on ping sweeps.
-sS
TCP SYN (half-open, "stealth") scan
Sends SYN. SYN/ACK means open, so Nmap replies RST and never finishes the handshake. RST/ACK means closed. No reply means filtered. Needs root.
Many SYN packets from one source to many ports, with RST packets back. Filter: tcp.flags.syn==1 && tcp.flags.ack==0.
Most applications do not log it, because no connection is fully made. Network IDS tools (for example Snort) detect the pattern.
-sT
TCP connect scan
Uses the operating system to finish the full three-way handshake, then closes it. Works without root.
Complete handshakes followed by RST/ACK or FIN.
Easier to see: servers and firewalls often log each full connection, for example in the web or SSH logs.
-sU
UDP scan
Sends UDP packets. An ICMP port-unreachable (type 3, code 3) reply means closed. No reply means open or filtered. Slow, so --top-ports is often used.
Many UDP packets to different ports and many ICMP unreachable replies.
Few application logs. Firewalls may log blocked UDP and ICMP.
-sV
Service/version detection
Connects to open ports and sends probes, then compares banners and replies to a database to find the service and version.
Full connections with unusual or malformed application requests.
Application logs show odd requests, strange User-Agents or protocol errors.
-O
OS detection
Sends specially made TCP, UDP and ICMP packets to an open and a closed port and compares the replies with known operating system fingerprints. Needs root.
Unusual packets: odd flag and option combinations, repeated probes.
IDS signatures often exist for these probes.
-A
Aggressive scan
Turns on -sV, -O, default scripts (-sC) and traceroute together.
All of the above in one noisy burst.
Very easy to detect, so use only on your own lab or with permission.


3. Useful related options
Option
Purpose
-Pn
Skip host discovery. Useful when a Windows firewall blocks ping.
-p- , -p1-1023 , -F
All ports, a port range, or the 100 most common ports.
-T0 to -T5
Timing speed. T0 is the slowest and quietest. T5 is the fastest and noisiest.
-oN, -oX, -oG, -oA
Save output as normal text, XML, grepable, or all three.
-v / -vv
More detail while scanning.


4. The defender's view
A SYN scan shows as many SYN packets and RSTs in a short time. Snort or Suricata rules count SYNs from one source over time.
Open ports in a scan result are what an attacker would try next, so defenders also scan their own systems to find and close unneeded ports.
Run Nmap only on systems you own or have written permission to test. In this lab, scan only the Ubuntu and Windows VMs.
Remember: SYN scan = half-open, quiet in application logs. Connect scan = full handshake, visible in logs. UDP scan = slow, relies on ICMP errors. -sV and -O are about identifying services and systems, and -A does everything at once.


